  <h2>When is authorisation required?</h2>
  <p>The French fire and panic safety regulations for public venues (ERP, établissements recevant du public) cover the case of <strong>exceptional use of premises</strong> (article GN 6). Authorisation is required when a venue is used, even partially or only once:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>for an activity other than the one it is authorised for;</li>
    <li>for a demonstration or attraction that may present risks to the public and that the regulations do not provide for.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Typical cases are a concert in an exhibition hall, an evening event in a museum or a fashion show in a venue that does not usually host performances.</p>

  <h2>What lead time should you allow?</h2>
  <p>The application must be submitted <strong>at least two months before</strong> the event. This is the wording of article GN 6 in force since 4 November 2023: older guides still give a shorter deadline. A single authorisation can cover a series of events over a period set by the organisers.</p>

  <h2>Who submits the application?</h2>
  <p>The venue <strong>operator</strong>. When the organiser is not the operator, the application is submitted <strong>jointly</strong> by the operator and the occasional user of the premises. Nothing prevents you from entrusting the writing of the safety report and the drawings to a specialist.</p>

  <h2>What must the application contain?</h2>
  <p>Article GN 6 lists what the application must always specify:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>the nature of the event;</li>
    <li>the risks it presents;</li>
    <li>its duration;</li>
    <li>its exact location;</li>
    <li>the expected attendance;</li>
    <li>the materials used for the planned decorations;</li>
    <li>the layout of the escape routes;</li>
    <li>the additional prevention and protection measures proposed.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>In practice, this information is gathered in a safety report accompanied by drawings (site layout, fit-out, escape routes).</p>

  <h2>And under a marquee or a tent?</h2>
  <p>Marquees, tents and structures open to the public fall under specific regulations, known as type <strong>CTS</strong> (amended order of 23 January 1985). Their regulatory inspections are carried out by <strong>accredited inspection bodies</strong> approved by order of the French Minister of the Interior. The documents specific to the marquee are supplied by its owner or hire company; its siting and operation must be described in the event file.</p>

  <h2>What ESE does</h2>
  <p>We write the safety report and the drawings, prepare the authorisation file and support the organiser at the safety commission, before the prefecture, the town hall and the fire and rescue service (SDIS). ESE's founder, Matthieu Savatier, holds the AP2 qualification from ENSOSP, the French national fire officers' academy. Regulatory inspections remain with the approved bodies, which we coordinate.</p>
